last week's dramatic events have brought  back memories gelatine Jean remembers  the military coup of 1980 very well he  was a student in Istanbul at the time to  this day he is haunted by the brutality  he experienced this is where they  arrested I was wedged between two cars  the police jumped out put a sack over me  to the police station there the  so-called interrogation began they beat  the soles of my feet administered  electric shocks he pried from me asleep  and shouted abuse at me for two days  some 650,000 people were arrested in the  course of a few days  they included left-wing activists like  Jang liberal thinkers and many Kurds the  push took place on September 12th 1980  falling a period of unrest between left  and right-wing groups martial law was  declared he stumbles Taksim Square  became a symbol of the coup last week  this square was once again occupied by  soldiers determined to overthrow the  government and seize power days later  people stopped to take photos here  thousands of AKP supporters answered air  to ones call to take to the streets to  defeat the coup our government and our  president we're strong together our  lives will now go on as normal  we're coming out of this stronger we  won't let anyone bring us down Elton  John is glad the coup was a failure but  he doesn't share the joy of a Diwan  supporters either he says this time it's  a battle between two authoritarian  forces in his view that wasn't the case  before 36 years ago the coup was not  against the government  but against a large portion of society  against those who were fighting for an  emancipated and democratic future  regardless of whether these people were  students or businesspeople the army  wanted to stop this development grief  and defiance as the civilian victims of  the attempted coup are laid to rest  Allah is great mourners cry some accuse  religious authorities of using the  funerals as an opportunity to display  their might in the cities AKP supporters  have been patrolling the streets at  night soldiers should never again use  their weapons against their own people  even if they couldn't be held  accountable they will never escape  punishment from Allah critics of the  government fear the failed coup could  cause Islamist forces to rally around  erawan further strengthening his grip on  power the attempted coup will make it  easier for Iran to impose his vision of  an authoritarian autocratic system  almost as bad for democracy as a  military coup for years Jon has been  fighting for the generals involved in  the 1980 coup to face justice as well as  the authorities who tortured him but to  no avail  still for John a military coup is never  the answer  whatever side you're on a military coup  is never a good thing  no matter what it's against in the end  it's always the progressive forces and  the Kurds who paid a price to this day  gelatine John continues to fight for his  beliefs opposing autocratic forces and  the military he spent 19 years in prison  following the push of 1980 but he  believes a military coup can never be  the answer not even in Turkey
 

